offlinecompiler: the image that was compiled must be the image the cache is named after.

`patches/fex/0011` closed this section with "binding a cache to the bytes it was
built from — not implemented", and named the question that would come back:
nothing in the cache format ties a cache to image contents. This is the answer,
and the answer is narrower than the question. **The runtime side is sound. The
generator side had no binding at all.**

WHAT THE KEY COVERS TODAY.

A cache file on the device is named
`advapi32.dll-e40a14e11a79bc89-0000000000000000`. The three fields:

| Field | Where | What |
|---|---|---|
| `advapi32.dll` | `FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/CodeCache.cpp:83-85` | `GetBaseFilename` — the basename of `ExecutableFileInfo::Filename` |
| `e40a14e11a79bc89` | `Source/Windows/Common/ImageTracker.cpp:39-42` | `FileId` = `XXH3_64bits(tolower(basename)) ^ (SizeOfImage << 32 | TimeDateStamp)` |
| `0000000000000000` | `Source/Windows/Common/ImageTracker.cpp:336` and `Source/Tools/FEXOfflineCompiler/Main.cpp:971-972` | `CodeCacheConfigId`, `uint64_t CodeCacheConfigId = 0; // TODO` — hardcoded zero on both the load and the generate side |

The `TimeDateStamp` and `SizeOfImage` are read from the PE headers of the image
as it was mapped (`ImageTracker.cpp:123`, `Nt->FileHeader.TimeDateStamp` and
`Nt->OptionalHeader.SizeOfImage`). The cache header itself carries no content
digest either — `CodeCacheHeader` (`CodeCache.cpp:285-301`) is `Magic`,
`FormatVersion`, `FEXVersion` (the FEX git hash), `NumBlocks`, `NumCodePages`,
`CodeBufferSize`, `NumRelocations`, `SerializedBaseAddress`, and nothing else.

The third field is not a gap Vessel has to close in FEX: `fexCacheKey`
(`app/src/main/java/app/vessel/core/SessionEnvironment.kt:790`) hashes the FEX
package identity and every `FEX_*` variable into the cache *directory* name, so
a codegen-affecting configuration change lands in a different directory. That is
already documented there and is not touched here.

THE HAZARD AS POSED — SELF-MODIFYING CODE — IS NOT REAL, AND HERE IS WHY.

The concern was that a Denuvo-style image decrypts itself and FEX then executes
translations of the encrypted bytes. Two independent facts rule it out.

**First, the cache is installed at a moment when it is provably in agreement with
memory.** `ImageTracker::HandleImageMap` runs inside the guest's
`NtMapViewOfSection` (`Source/Windows/ARM64EC/Module.cpp:955-963`), before the
call returns and therefore before the guest has executed one instruction of the
new image. The bytes at that instant are the file's bytes, which is exactly what
`FEXOfflineCompiler` compiled — it maps the same file with `SEC_IMAGE`
(`Main.cpp:323`). So the cache is correct when it is published, and every later
divergence is a *write*.

**Second, every write is tracked, and the cache's blocks are invalidated by the
same mechanism as JIT blocks.** `EnableLoadedSection` registers cached
translations through the ordinary `LookupCache` API — `AddBlockMapping` and
`AddBlockExecutableRange` (`CodeCache.cpp:969` and `:992`) — into
`CTX.GetLatest()->LookupCache` (`:956-959`). `InvalidationTracker` funnels every
write path into `InvalidateIntervalInternalLocked` →
`ContextImpl::InvalidateCodeBuffersCodeRange` (`Core.cpp:969-983`) →
`GuestToHostMap::InvalidateRange` (`LookupCache.h:125-137`), which erases
entries by walking `CodePages` — the same map `AddBlockExecutableRange` just
populated. It cannot tell a cached block from a compiled one, which is the
property that matters.

The write paths, all of them:

| Path | Where |
|---|---|
| `NtProtectVirtualMemory` | `ARM64EC/Module.cpp:940-953` → `HandleMemoryProtectionNotification` |
| `NtAllocateVirtualMemory` | `ARM64EC/Module.cpp:909-923` |
| `NtFreeVirtualMemory` | `ARM64EC/Module.cpp:925-938` |
| `NtUnmapViewOfSection` | `ARM64EC/Module.cpp:969-983` |
| `NtReadFile` into guest memory | `ARM64EC/Module.cpp:1012-1035` |
| `BTCpu64FlushInstructionCache` / `NotifyMemoryDirty` | `ARM64EC/Module.cpp:994-1010` |
| a plain store to an RWX page | `InvalidationTracker.cpp:308-445`, `HandleRWXAccessViolation` |

A `.text` section is mapped `PAGE_EXECUTE_READ`, so a decryptor must call
`NtProtectVirtualMemory` before it can write — row one — and that alone
invalidates the range before a single decrypted byte lands. With `DisableDEP`
(`patches/fex/0002`) the net is wider still: `HandleMemoryProtectionNotification`
treats any readable page as executable (`InvalidationTracker.cpp:145-146`), so
even a plain `PAGE_READWRITE` transition invalidates.

So the decryption case self-heals: the cache is dropped before the decrypted code
can run, and the block is retranslated from the bytes that are actually there.
`0011` was right that the theory could not be that crash; it is also not a
correctness bug on the load path.

THE ON-DISK CASE IS COVERED TOO, AND THE EVIDENCE IS MEASURABLE.

`(basename, TimeDateStamp, SizeOfImage)` is the tuple Windows itself uses as a
binary's identity — it is the symbol-server key — and any relink moves the
timestamp. Measured on Vessel's own shipped artifacts, every PE carries a real
per-build timestamp, seconds apart within one build:

```
wine-10.13   aarch64  advapi32.dll   TimeDateStamp=0x6a75be93  SizeOfImage=0xe0000
wine-11.14   aarch64  advapi32.dll   TimeDateStamp=0x6a7d8054  SizeOfImage=0xf0000
wine-10.13   aarch64  ntdll.dll      TimeDateStamp=0x6a75be97  SizeOfImage=0x1b0000
wine-11.14   aarch64  ntdll.dll      TimeDateStamp=0x6a7d805b  SizeOfImage=0x1b0000   <- same size
wine-10.13   aarch64  user32.dll     TimeDateStamp=0x6a75be97  SizeOfImage=0x2e0000
wine-11.14   aarch64  user32.dll     TimeDateStamp=0x6a7d805c  SizeOfImage=0x2e0000   <- same size
dxvk-2.7.1   x86-64   d3d11.dll      TimeDateStamp=0x6a7a9bf7  SizeOfImage=0x3ef000
vkd3d-3.0.1  x86-64   d3d12core.dll  TimeDateStamp=0x6a8119ec  SizeOfImage=0x4ee000
```

Note `ntdll.dll` and `user32.dll`: `SizeOfImage` is page-granular and is
*identical* across two Wine releases eight days apart. The timestamp is the only
field carrying the distinction. That matters for the next section.

WHERE THE BINDING IS ACTUALLY MISSING: THE GENERATOR NEVER CHECKS WHAT IT OPENED.

`TryMapImage` (`Main.cpp:313-348`) takes

```
static std::optional<uint64_t> TryMapImage(… , const FEXCore::CodeMapFileId& ID, FEXCore::ExecutableFileInfo& Info)
```

and **never reads `ID`**. It opens `Info.Filename` — a path recorded in a code
map, possibly weeks earlier — maps it, and returns. The caller then compiles the
block offsets that code map recorded and writes the result as
`{basename}-{ID:016x}-0000000000000000` (`Main.cpp:596`, with `ID` coming from
the code map at `:731-732`). Nothing anywhere compares the id of the image that
was actually opened against the id the output is named after.
`ProcessAll`'s only freshness test (`Main.cpp:1011`) compares `last_write_time`
of the cache against `last_write_time` of the code map and never looks at the
image at all.

**This produces a cache that describes bytes that were never at those addresses,
and it needs no Denuvo.** The path in a code map is a *guest* path —
`c:\windows\system32\advapi32.dll` for a Wine builtin. Vessel ships four Wine
builds (`dist/wine-10.13`, `wine-11.14`, `wine-proton-11.0`,
`wine-proton-exp-11.0`), each installing a different `advapi32.dll` at that path.
`fexCacheKey` keys the cache directory on the FEX package and the `FEX_*`
environment — deliberately not on the Wine package, see
`SessionEnvironment.kt:742-800` — so switching Wine inside a container keeps
`codemap/ready/` and `cache/` exactly where they were, and changes the bytes
behind that path. The sequence:

1. Session on wine-10.13 records `codemap/ready/advapi32.dll-<id10.13>`, holding
   block offsets into 10.13's `advapi32.dll` and the guest path.
2. User switches the container to wine-11.14. Cache directory unchanged.
3. `process-all` finds that code map, opens the path, gets **11.14's**
   `advapi32.dll`, compiles 10.13's block offsets out of it, and writes
   `cache/advapi32.dll-<id10.13>-0000000000000000`.
4. User switches back to wine-10.13. The runtime computes `<id10.13>` from the
   mapped image, finds that file, loads it, and executes it.

Every block in it is an entry point into the middle of an unrelated instruction
stream. Nothing on the load path can notice: `LoadCache`'s validation
(`patches/fex/0011`) checks the cache file's internal structure, which is
perfectly well-formed, and the invalidation machinery only reacts to writes,
which never happen — the bytes were already wrong when the cache was installed.
This is the one case where the second fact in the "not real" section above does
not hold, because the premise it rests on — that the compiler saw the same file —
is false.

The same shape reaches a game patch that rewrites a DLL and a user restoring a
backup; the Wine swap is simply the one Vessel makes a first-class operation.

THE FIX, AND WHY IT IS THE CHEAP ONE.

`ImageTracker::HandleImageMap` **already computes** the id of the image it just
mapped, from that image's own headers, and returns it in the
`ExecutableFileSectionInfo` it hands back. It is the same value the runtime will
compute when it goes looking for a cache. So the binding costs one comparison of
two `uint64_t` that are already in hand: no hashing of image bytes, no extra
I/O, nothing that scales with a 60 MB `libxess.dll`. On mismatch the compile is
refused; `GenerateSingleCache` returns `std::nullopt` and the child exits 1,
which is a path `ProcessAll` already handles.

A second, redundant check goes in `ProcessAll` itself, reading `TimeDateStamp`
and `SizeOfImage` straight out of the file's headers. It is not there for
correctness — the child's check is authoritative, because it sees the image as
mapped and relocated. It is there for cost: without it, a replaced image is
retried on *every* run forever, because the refusal means no cache file is
written, so the `last_write_time` test never becomes true, so the next
`process-all` spawns the same doomed child again for every affected module —
and Vessel runs `process-all` beside every session. Reading two `uint32_t` from
a file beats spawning a process. `SizeOfImage` sits at optional-header offset 56
in PE32 and PE32+ alike (PE32's extra 4-byte `BaseOfData` is exactly offset by
PE32+'s wider `ImageBase`), so one reader serves both bitnesses, which the
parent needs because it handles modules of both before choosing a child.

Refusal rather than renaming the output to the mapped image's id: the block
offsets come from the *old* image's code map, so compiled against the new image
they are not a valid cache for either. The correct code map for the new image is
produced by the next session that runs it, which is the only source that can
know.

WHAT THIS DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO.

It binds a generated cache to the image *identity* the runtime checks, not to the
image *bytes*. Two distinct byte-streams sharing a basename, a `TimeDateStamp`
and a `SizeOfImage` still pass. A content digest in `CodeCacheHeader` would close
that, and it was rejected on cost: it has to be recomputed at every module load,
on every launch, for every cached module, and the cost is not the hash — XXH3
runs at several GB/s — but the paging. Verifying a digest means touching every
code page of the image, which forces a 60 MB module fully resident at load time
and defeats the demand paging that makes a large module cheap to map on a phone.
That is a real, permanent, per-launch tax to defend against a collision that
requires a second-accurate timestamp match, and the measurements above say Vessel
ships nothing that produces one. `0011`'s other candidate — refusing to cache any
image with writable executable sections — is not needed at all now that the
invalidation argument above is written down.

If a title is ever found where this matters, the cheap escalation is to digest
only the PE headers plus the first and last page of each executable section,
which is bounded by section count rather than by image size. It is not correct in
general and so is not shipped speculatively.

